name  = axpy_simd
shape = the axpy row with explicit f64x2 lanes: two elements per step, 100 passes, 2,000,000 elements
args  = 2000000
cflags = -ffp-contract=off
claim = explicit lanes are for what the auto-vectorizer cannot do; this row
        measures what they cost where it CAN. The axpy row's loop is vectorized
        by clang once range-check versioning has removed the per-element
        checks (v0.1.420): VF 2, interleave 4 -- eight doubles per iteration.
        This row writes the same loop with f64x2_load / f64x2_mul / f64x2_add /
        f64x2_store, one vector (two doubles) per step, and the versioning pass
        covers the stride and the two-lane access (v0.1.423), so its loop body
        is the minimal one -- two loads, fmul, fadd, one store, the counter --
        and it is still slower: 0.12 s against the auto-vectorized 0.08 s and
        C's 0.07 s at v0.1.423, because two lanes per step is a quarter of the
        work in flight. Unrolling the Mere source by hand to four doubles per
        step gives 0.10 s. So the honest expectation for this row is BEHIND
        axpy and C by about 1.5x, and the day it ties is the day this backend
        learned to unroll (or the kernel was written wider). The number that
        justifies explicit lanes is utf8valid_simd, not this one.

# The same two Vecs as axpy: 36,700,448 B at v0.1.420. Nothing else allocates.
alloc_min = 33554432
alloc_max = 50331648
